ptember consistently show El-Sayed ahead, with high-quality pollsters like Marist, Siena, and Suffolk giving him leads of five to seven points, while some Republican-leaning pollsters show the race tied or closer. The polling average sits around 3.5 points for El-Sayed.\n\nOutside groups have poured money into the race, with heavy spending opposing El-Sayed, and prediction markets price his chances near 72%. Most expert raters call the race Lean or Tilt Democratic, though a few still rate it a tossup. The state's even partisan baseline keeps this competitive, but the weight of polling and market evidence points to a modest Democratic advantage heading into the final weeks.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.7769, "margin": 3.59}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.7646, "margin": 3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.7534, "margin": 3.2}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.7553, "margin": 3.4}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.6955, "margin": 3.2}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.7118, "margin": 3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.7565, "margin": 3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.7778, "margin": 3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.755, "margin": 3.5}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.7606, "margin": 3.5}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.7617, "margin": 3.5}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.7781, "margin": 3.5}]}